Translation patch: RistrettoPoint::double_and_compress_batch (src/ristretto.rs)

Change: `points.into_iter().map(BatchCompressState::from).collect()` becomes a nested helper `states`;
`states.iter().map(|state| state.efgh()).collect()` and the final
`states.iter().zip(invs.iter()).map(..).collect()` become index loops pushing into `Vec`s.
The body of the final closure is unchanged apart from indentation (it is now the loop body,
ending in `out.push(..)`). The Charon config excludes the derived `Debug` impl of the local
`BatchCompressState`, which is unrelated to this patch.

Why: the Aeneas Lean library models only `Iterator::next`; the provided
methods (`map`, `zip`, `fold`, `filter`, `collect`, `rev`/`skip` on adapters, ...)
are missing (AeneasVerif/aeneas#1057), so the original does not build in Lean.
Aeneas also cannot extract a loop that calls `next()` directly on the
`IntoIter` of a generic `IntoIterator` parameter ("Could not find: type_var_id ..
from ExtractBase.Item"), so the consuming loop lives in a nested helper function
generic over `It: Iterator`, called with `x.into_iter()`.

Behaviour: identical: the helper calls `next()` until `None` and pushes the mapped item for each
element, in order, which is exactly what `.map(f).collect::<Vec<_>>()` does. the `zip` becomes an index loop over `0..states.len()`, visiting the
same pairs in the same order (`zip` stops at the shorter side). Here both have length `states.len()`.

Remove this patch when Aeneas translates the original code: delete the file,
run ./scripts/aeneas-translate.sh and `lake build`, and check both succeed.

